nd it's hard to  avoid products that are give one get one  you know in use bono to save the world  somehow by buying an iPhone case and  what is remarkable is that this period  that is so awash in these forms of elite  change has also been a period that is  basically punishing for the working and  middle classes in this country and so  you have a situation where the rich and  powerful are talking a lot about doing  well by doing good giving back making a  difference but they are building and  operating and maintaining a system that  keeps them on top locks most people out  and so the kind of change that they  peddle is fake change in my view it's  change that does not change their own  position it's changed it does not change  the social arrangements that allow them  to win and and keep others out it's the  kind of change that allows you know  their own world to not change at all and  the kind of generosity that allows  injustice to fester we've been talking a  lot on my show and I think some in my  audience disagree with my position on  the sort of use of techniques like the  heckling of politicians in public we can  debate whether we think that's useful or  not or whatever whatever activism  technique we want to talk about and one  of the points I've been making is that  if you stick to doing activism and  lobbying for change only in the ways  that those in power are comfortable with  you're immediately conceding that the  scope of the change you can achieve is  much smaller than what it is that that  may actually need to happen or that you  may be targeting and for me it's a sort  of similar thing with corporations and  that when you hear an announcement about  major giving campaigns that some huge  multinational is going to be doing we  focus on well what exactly will that  money do and it becomes almost  background that it will maintain the  exact infrastructure that allowed that  corporation to consolidate assets and  power in this way and I'm curious to  hear