t the  narrative around Medicare for all is of  this extremist idea that barely deserves  attention look I I I'm not sure it's  always because I mean the corporate  media includes many different things you  know I think there there there is some  media that because it is corporate is  certain ideas but I think the problem  when you look at the really great papers  and magazines the problem is not their  corporate ownership I think it's  actually something having worked at the  New York Times for many years having you  know known a lot of people within that  media there is a kind of real wariness  and skepticism of political power and a  lot of the self-image of journalists was  shaped by Watergate in this country and  the idea that people with political  power need to be held to account  investigated etc and of course those  publications also investigate the  private sector and business but I think  often with these kind of entrepreneur  hero figures the coverage has just been  a little puffier you know I think if you  think about something like Goldman Sachs  or you know you know Facebook and  Cambridge analytic ah like you end up  getting very serious scrutiny but on the  way up a company like uber or Facebook  while it's still growing has a little  bit more of a free ride even though it's  exerting an enormous amount of power and  you know when you have these people  Steve Case is starting an impact fund  and that's not often it goes straight to  here is how this person is trying to  make things better instead of why do  they have that much money what is what  tax measures of these other tax measures  that they are using or are those  creating harm to this society on a scale  that Dwarfs the good that they're doing  in other words writing about rich people  the way we write about senators and  congressmen which is always with a  gimlet eye always trying to understand  what's behind and I you know I do think  there's a way in which we've turned the  entrepreneur king of our age into these  kind of swashbuckling heroes that has  created space for them to to rule us  instead of just being part of the mix on  that note I mean it in what way does  this relate to the rise of Trump the  concept of fake  the coverage of these corporate sort of  pseudo heroes as they are perceived by  some and what's the relationship there I  think an uncomfortable truth is that the  plutocratic kind of philanthropy a  fabulous class pave the way for Donald  Trump in two important ways the first  way they did so is by aggressively  peddling non solutions to grave problems  for 30 or 40 years as social mobility  was vanishing from the United States as  the education system was failing to  prepare people for a new world of  technology and automation and and  globalization as you had the shift from  a industrial economy to a service  economy etc etc etc we just didn't do  any of the really big things we needed  to do to prepare people for the new  world and meanwhile we told women to  lean in we both the Charter School here  and there we did the kind of you know we  put billionaires names on public  libraries while not actually funding  public libraries more we did all the  kind of pseudo changes that that created  the kind of the illusion of change  without without the reality so those  philanthropists created space for Donald  Trump by not solving things that he  could then point to and say like  America's not Great America can be made  great again now of course what he meant  by that was also enormous ly tinged by  outright racism but the racism and  economics work together in the sense  that this country is rigged it does not  work for people anymore is something  that actually unites people on the left  and right people of color white people  women and men and he tapped into both  the racial undercurrents and that sense  of things being rigged in a way that was  incredibly powerful the second way those  people paved the way for him is they  gave h